# Lazynext Slop Guard (Cursor rules)

Company: Lazynext · UNITED GLOBAL VENTURES PRIVATE LIMITED · support@lazynext.com · https://lazynext.com

## When this applies

You are editing or reviewing prose, product copy, docs, or UI markup that a
model (or a hurried human) may have written. Before calling it done, check it
against the tells below. Every tell is a *default nobody chose* — a deliberate
choice is not slop and must be preserved.

## Writing tells — cut or fix

1. **Banned vocabulary.** Remove or replace: delve, foster, leverage, utilize,
   facilitate, empower, streamline, robust, cutting-edge, paradigm shift,
   game-changer, tapestry, realm, beacon, multifaceted, meticulous, intricate,
   paramount, transformative, elevate, embark, supercharge, harness,
   ever-evolving, seamless, unlock, journey, synergy, next-level,
   revolutionize, unleash, next-generation, industry-leading, future-proof
   enterprise-grade, this is huge. Name the mechanism instead.
2. **Empty phrases.** Delete: "it's worth noting", "it's important to note",
   "at the end of the day", "at its core", "in today's world", "in the age of",
   "in the world of", "in a world where", "the reality is", "the truth is",
   "when it comes to", "in terms of", "with regard to", "in order to",
   "going forward", "let's dive in", "it turns out", "more than just".
3. **Throat-clearing / faux insight.** Delete: "here's the thing",
   "here's why", "here's the problem though", "let me be clear", "i'll be
   honest", "the uncomfortable truth is", "what nobody tells you", "what
   most people get wrong", "the part everyone misses". State the claim
   directly.
4. **Rhythm crutches.** Binary contrasts ("It's not X. It's Y." plus "Not
   because X. Because Y.", "stops being X and starts being Y", "doesn't mean
   X, but actually Y"), colon reveals ("The best part: it learns."), negative
   listings ("Not a X. Not a Y. A Z."), dramatic fragments ("X. And Y. And
   Z."), em-dash clusters (3+ dashes), rhetorical setups ("What if I told
   you", "And that's okay"), stacked one-liners. Rewrite as plain, varied
   prose.
5. **Puffery and weasel words.** "stands as a testament", "marks a pivotal
   moment", "plays a vital role", "experts agree", "studies show" — cut or
   cite. Empty adverbs (really, just, literally, genuinely, simply, actually,
   truly, fundamentally, importantly, crucially, inherently, inevitably,
   arguably, deeply) — cut when they add nothing.
6. **Superficial analysis.** Trailing -ing clauses that name a feeling:
   "highlighting the team's commitment", "underscoring the importance",
   "reflecting our focus", "showcasing our dedication". Say what the change
   does.
7. **Meta-commentary.** "the key point is", "this distinction matters", "as
   you can see", "let that sink in", "make no mistake", "this matters
   because", "the rest of this essay", "in this section we'll". Delete the
   aside.
8. **Vague declaratives.** "the stakes are high", "the implications are
   significant", "the reasons are structural". Name the specific thing.
9. **False agency and passive voice.** "the decision emerges", "the data
   tells us", "the market rewards", "nobody designed this"; "it is believed
   that", "mistakes were made", "the decision was reached". Name the actor.
10. **Formatting slop.** Emoji in headings, decorative bold, bullets where
    prose belongs, headers over two-sentence sections.

## Design tells — UI in this diff

1. **Colour:** indigo-violet gradients (`from-indigo-500 to-violet-600`,
   `#6366f1`→`#a855f7`), gradient headline text (`bg-clip-text
   text-transparent`), colored glow shadows, warm amber/stone "cozy" washes,
   the stock semantic rainbow (bg-blue-50/amber-50/green-50/red-50 pairs),
   one-hue status boxes (border+text+bg /10 all the same hue), glow halos.
2. **Type:** one serif-italic word inside a sans headline, display serifs
   (Playfair/Lora/Cormorant) in UI, a tracked-caps kicker above every heading
   (`uppercase tracking-widest`), h1 at `text-lg`/`text-base` (flat
   hierarchy), full-sentence display headlines (`text-6xl font-extrabold
   tracking-tight`), crushed tracking (`letter-spacing: -0.06em`), decorative
   strikes (`line-through`/`<mark>`/`<s>` for emphasis), the editorial
   dashboard ("Good morning," serif greeting, oldstyle numerals), default
   Inter/Space Grotesk pairings with no stated reason.
3. **Components:** `animate-ping` status dots, `border-l-4` callouts on every
   list item, rounded-square icon tiles, giant decorative icons (`w-24 h-24`),
   `backdrop-blur` glass surfaces, `rounded-full` pills on cards, badge/pill
   spam ("✨ New", "β Beta"), `bg-blue-50` icon-in-a-tint-of-itself, huge soft
   drop shadows, ghost cards (hairline + wide shadow), hard offset shadows
   (`box-shadow: 4px 4px 0`), "Powered by AI" badges, blob/mascot SVG marks,
   tasteful-terminal mono+ASCII costumes.
4. **Motion:** `hover:scale-105` + `transition-all` on everything, spinners
   whose rotate keyframe clobbers a translate(-50%,-50%) centre (wobble),
   marquee/ticker strips, reveal classes hiding content at rest with no `.js`
   gate.
5. **Layout:** invented stat rows (`10k+ users`, `99.9% uptime`, `24/7`),
   `01/02/03` markers on unordered sections, cards nested in cards, one
   spacing value stamped everywhere (`gap-4`/`space-y-4` × N), equal-weight
   card grids with nothing leading, codex-grid hairline backgrounds, border
   that dies at a rounded corner (`rounded-xl overflow-hidden` wrapper +
   child border), "Built for the modern team"/"In today's digital age"
   boilerplate, middle-dot meta strings ("A · B · C") or glued arrows
   ("Learn more →").

## Quality floor

- Lead with the point when the setup adds nothing.
- Specific beats punchy: names, numbers, dates, mechanisms, consequences.
- Portability test: if a sentence could move to any other product unchanged,
  it is filler — cut it or make it specific.
- Active voice with human subjects. The team shipped it Tuesday beats the
  decision emerged.
- Show, don't tell. No "this highlights our commitment to".
- One accent, one voice. Subtract first.
- Preserve the user's deliberate choices (brand tokens, a logo, a real
  illustration) — that is what separates taste from slop.

## Verification

When a scan tool is available (repo root):

```sh
node api/cli.js --file <file>     # text; exit 2 = findings
node api/cli.js --url <url>       # remote HTML
node api/cli.js --dir <path>      # whole-project scan; exit 2 = findings
node api/cli.js --json <text>     # machine-readable report
```

If any of the notes above apply to this diff, fix the tell (or, for a
deliberate choice, leave it and say why in your reply). For a project scan,
triage each hit (confirmed or deliberate?), report the grouped findings
before changing anything, fix, and re-scan until the count drops. Sign off
with the file name and which tells were fixed.